{
 "refId": "fitting-dimensions",
 "version": "1.2.0",
 "source": "ASME B16.9-2018, Factory-Made Wrought Buttwelding Fittings, Tables 6.1-1 and 6.1-10 (SI). Transcribed from a purchased copy held outside version control.",
 "note": "The other half of closing an isometric. B16.10 gave the length of a valve; this gives the take-out of an elbow, which is what actually sets the cut length of the pipe between two of them.",
 "units": {
  "length": "mm"
 },
 "conventions": {
  "aButtweldingFittingHasNoClass": "Clause 1.5: \"Fitting pressure rating is associated with the connecting wall thickness of pipe of equivalent size and material.\" Clause 2.1: the allowable pressure \"may be calculated as for straight seamless pipe of equivalent material ... in accordance with ASME B31\". A butt-welding fitting therefore carries NO pressure class of its own - it is rated as the pipe it is welded to, and for this platform that calculation is the B31.3 chain in WallThickness and AllowableStress. Asking a PMS row for an elbow rating is asking for something the standard does not issue.",
  "keyedOnOutsideDiameter": "Rows are keyed on the outside diameter at bevel and the NPS recovered from pipe-sizes, because the size column suffers the vulgar-fraction split that has bitten every dimensional standard read here: \"1 1/4\" arrives as a bare \"1\" on one line and \"1/4\" on the next.",
  "theRuleAndItsFloor": "A long radius elbow is 1.5 x NPS centre to end and a 45 degree one is 0.625 x NPS - which is how these figures were checked rather than trusted. The rule does NOT hold at the small end: B16.9 sets a FLOOR, so an NPS 1/2 and an NPS 3/4 long radius elbow are both 38 mm (1.50 in) where the rule would give 19 and 29. The floor is the standard, not a transcription fault, and a consumer that computes 1.5 x NPS instead of reading the table will under-dimension every small elbow on the drawing.",
  "centreToEndNotLength": "An elbow is measured CENTRE TO END, not end to end: the dimension runs from the intersection of the two centrelines to the welding bevel. Treating it as a length makes every spool between two elbows short by twice the radius.",
  "capLengthApplicability": "Table 6.1-10 notes (1)/(2): for NPS 1/2 through 24 select E/E1 from explicitly sourced specified thickness. NPS 26 through 48 are WITHIN B16.9 coverage: E is tabulated but its thickness threshold and E1 are not. E1 requires manufacturer/purchaser agreement; do not choose E by assuming a missing threshold means infinity, or infer E1.",
  "capSourceReview": "Threshold and E1 cells cross-checked against the user-supplied ASME B16.9-2018 PDF, SHA-256 f254b756cf54dc4115fba10c659f205cee913c816068736ee76f59b608052ab4; printed page 20 / PDF page 31. This is a limited transcription review, not engineering certification.",
  "sizeScope": "B16.9-2018 clause 1.1: NPS 1/2 through 48. Reference ingestion gaps do not narrow this range. Outside this range, the user must define dimensions according to the project specification, with separate project provenance, not B16.9 extrapolation."
 },
